Subject: [PATCH] linux: verify the kernel tarball we actually pin

The kernel is the one download in this build that Buildroot does not
insist on verifying, and until now it was verified only by luck.

linux/linux.hash carries one entry per maintained series -- for 6.12,
the point release the Buildroot tarball was cut against, .104 on
2025.02.17. That happened to be the version we pinned, so the sum was
found and checked. Move the pin one point release along and the entry no
longer matches, and the interesting part is what does NOT happen: the
build does not stop. linux.mk has

  ifeq ($(BR2_LINUX_KERNEL)$(BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_LATEST_VERSION),y)
  BR_NO_CHECK_HASH_FOR += $(LINUX_SOURCE)

which reads as "the latest-version kernel is exempt" and means the
opposite: with BR2_LINUX_KERNEL=y and LATEST_VERSION unset -- that is,
with a custom version, which is exactly our configuration -- the two
expand to "y" and the tarball goes on the exemption list. Buildroot says
so itself:

  $ make printvars VARS=BR_NO_CHECK_HASH_FOR | tr ' ' '\n' | grep linux
  linux-6.12.109.tar.xz

support/download/check-hash consults that list only when it found no sum
to check against, so the exemption is invisible while a sum exists and
silently disables verification the moment one does not. A 148 MB kernel
would be fetched over the network and unpacked with nothing checking it,
and the build would go green.

So name our version's sum. It goes at the end of the file, after the
license hashes, rather than beside Buildroot's own 6.12 entry: that
entry moves with every Buildroot point release, and a hunk anchored on
it would either stop applying or -- worse, because scripts/configure.sh
runs a plain `patch` at its default fuzz of 2 -- land somewhere
plausible and wrong. The three license hashes have not moved in years
and make a duller, safer anchor.

The sum is from the PGP-signed sha256sums.asc kernel.org publishes for
v6.x, not read off a mirror and not copied out of a build log. Proven by
setting it wrong first, so the check had to object --

  ERROR: while checking hashes from linux/linux.hash
  ERROR: linux-6.12.109.tar.xz has wrong sha256 hash:
  ERROR: expected: 0000e552a334e15019f4aeba89e5b58f04651cf2f4e24e04de9f152f1c38e3fa
  ERROR: got     : 5484e552a334e15019f4aeba89e5b58f04651cf2f4e24e04de9f152f1c38e3fa
  ERROR: Incomplete download, or man-in-the-middle (MITM) attack

-- which is also an independent confirmation: that "got" line is
Buildroot's own sha256 of the tarball it pulled from cdn.kernel.org, and
it is the value in the signed file. With the real sum in place:

  linux-6.12.109.tar.xz: OK (sha256: 5484e552a334e15019f4aeba89e5b58f04651cf2f4e24e04de9f152f1c38e3fa)

Not fixed here: BR2_DOWNLOAD_FORCE_CHECK_HASHES empties the exemption
list globally and would turn every unverified download into an error.
It would also break this tree today, because the five git-sourced
packages (bmcd, tpi, bmc_installer, bmc-ui, u-boot) have no hash files
and rely on the same exemption. Worth doing; a separate change.

---
 linux/linux.hash | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

--- a/linux/linux.hash
+++ b/linux/linux.hash
@@ -14,3 +14,11 @@
 sha256  fb5a425bd3b3cd6071a3a9aff9909a859e7c1158d54d32e07658398cd67eb6a0  COPYING
 sha256  f6b78c087c3ebdf0f3c13415070dd480a3f35d8fc76f3d02180a407c1c812f79  LICENSES/preferred/GPL-2.0
 sha256  8e378ab93586eb55135d3bc119cce787f7324f48394777d00c34fa3d0be3303f  LICENSES/exceptions/Linux-syscall-note
+
+# The version this board pins (BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_VERSION_VALUE in
+# tp2bmc_defconfig). Buildroot lists only the point release it defaults
+# to, and linux.mk puts a custom-version tarball in BR_NO_CHECK_HASH_FOR
+# -- so with no line here the kernel is downloaded and NOT verified, and
+# nothing says so. Name it and the check runs.
+# From https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/sha256sums.asc
+sha256  5484e552a334e15019f4aeba89e5b58f04651cf2f4e24e04de9f152f1c38e3fa  linux-6.12.109.tar.xz
